trix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166800 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166800 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1039 "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With a Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166801 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Zero or Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167041 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5d719a4c-ab32-5d8b-b2a7-90e293dc6083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167634 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167634 #edb/ref 1061 31418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5235213b-71c0-5372-9b7e-3f7b5abf5c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167635 #edb/ref 1061 31419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8cbe873-c1e3-5e8a-a689-8e8116d5b70e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167636 #edb/ref 1061 31420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86c7507c-0f40-558d-b0f8-9cb4d481e270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167637 #edb/ref 1061 31421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af4cc601-95a5-50bc-8b95-471cf44bc75f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167657 #edb/ref 1061 31529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "967ac37b-6901-5b91-988c-19fc53224cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168070 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ If the $i$th singular value of $A$ is nonzero, then the $i$th column of the orthogonal matrix $U$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nThe formula above works whenever $\\sigma_i\\neq 0.$ But how do we find the $i$th column of $U$ in cases where $\\sigma_i = 0?$ It turns out that we can find the corresponding left singular vector using the geometric properties of singular values.\n\nFor example, suppose that\n\n![](images/fa/fab8a223fc22100592865eeb8b147313f4a635ffa3791863826ca6330730cc58.svg)\n\nand $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ where\n![](images/58/58af793e95fd96171530b2b63e74d12e965fb02eb81af42c8fdb3c9289a04b11.svg)\nThe matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ can be found using the methods we've seen before. So, our task now is to find the matrix $U.$ \n\nFrom $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ However, we know that $U$ is an orthogonal matrix. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/78/781532700996b7a60101f204b934483da264ecab6acd6bfeda97141669308b6c.svg)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider each condition in turn:\n\n- The equation $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$  is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{2}} x_1 +  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} x_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSeeking a nonzero solution to this equation, we get the vector \n![](images/75/7521799052487c5668d209c97ad6779f4258ecc5710ac81f2135966bf39974d3.svg)\n\n- Normalizing the vector we just found, we get\n![](images/30/30cc0b825690ed4dcd0bd672563f37bc6e621be092db70c4b46dafbf22db10c4.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal matrix $U$ is given by\n\n![](images/92/92ca53d48aed0c8435f89db4e11c022bc27ecb7a13d741e03270e0c981f639ef.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168070 #edb/ref 1065 5024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168070 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "01e70dc7-d85e-5e8c-939b-e7cd2c8b9ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168070 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168073 #edb/ref 1064 "It follows from the definitions that a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two repeated singular values $\\sigma_1= \\sigma_2= \\sigma$ if and only if the matrix  $A^TA$ has two repeated eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2 = \\sigma^2.$  Thus, since $A^TA$ is diagonalizable (because it is symmetric), the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2$ must be two-dimensional. Therefore, \n\n![](images/df/df266614e2576526353248439b7a17431d5e9e43153e1ba225adab3a89321373.svg)\n\nConsequently, we can use any pair of unit orthogonal vectors as a basis for the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nLet's illustrate this with an example. Consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ee/ee74c3d4ed7e32388d06b47ed79789d768b54dee61e64d6db57c3eeb582089ae.svg)\n\nLet's find a singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since $A^T \\! A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$): Since the singular values are equal, we can always take as solutions of $(A^T\\!A-13I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/d1/d1770843817b2557b032743e186dd27106374fef90a2a8e8bb3cfe77b2eb0398.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c950cf3aff8f9ab47ede762fa9c4451c5e7fc1e78e12fca064ee87d89fcb571e.svg)\n\n**Note:** By swapping the order of eigenvectors in step 3, we swap the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2$ in step 4. However, the resulting matrices (with swapped columns) will give us a valid singular decomposition too." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168073 #edb/ref 1065 5040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168073 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9a6bbc33-1584-5b2f-aab5-628e9c95743a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168073 #edb/ref 1067 "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6da9341-93f3-5154-bb0f-938a2383bb07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371353 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371354 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "568ea8b9-1e08-5c04-9b16-5b0f11806d6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371355 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e66d06cf-403a-5e08-92e6-6e31654cb2f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371356 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "006adb9d-7d91-5c92-8d26-391d7b8d5193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371357 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371357 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371357 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ff9efa0-db92-59f9-b4b7-d17a1e3f87a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371358 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22ebb34c-6ef2-5e46-9297-d5664b1ea78e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371359 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371359 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371359 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b270d07a-2653-54b8-987d-795576fa2ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0e502e2-3a05-5080-ba55-d3ba7ba5d628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371388 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371389 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db5a28ad-71bd-5130-9bc2-0a90bb411ead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371390 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90fb4001-d74b-5e2d-81b4-e24b882a4776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371391 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371391 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371391 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71ce610f-82ae-522c-926e-b7c2ee6f0d39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371392 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb