255 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{40}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-40I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{40}}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{40}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{40}}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{40}}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$ \n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{40} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{40}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6fc6d8a-4eb7-5088-83d3-79243011f5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434256 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=25,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-20 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434257 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3868de93-336f-5be1-b8c0-ee5d1d36c94c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434257 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186371388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434258 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186434260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434258 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad6e7d37-c775-5b7b-b349-eb9dee4a1453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434258 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186434256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186434257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bce2f3bf-424e-54b0-b537-ee61a51b4d2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186434255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434260 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186434261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2f6c29c-1b1d-523c-bac9-0e9e225514e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186371353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434261 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186434259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434261 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb53c4bf-fd89-5cbd-9fad-7e1f192d2578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434261 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404878 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
